# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Obadiah'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'prophets'
SUBTITLE = 'Edom Brought Down, and Zion’s Kingdom the LORD’s'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Obadiah is a single chapter, “The vision of Obadiah” against Edom (Obad. 1). Pride in the clefts of the rock is brought down, Edom’s violence against his brother Jacob is named, the day of the LORD is near upon all the heathen, and deliverance is promised on mount Zion, where “the kingdom shall be the LORD’s” (Obad. 1:3–4, 10, 15, 17, 21).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'This book belongs entirely to the Prophecy Program, where the LORD speaks of Edom’s fall, of Jacob and the house of Joseph, and of deliverance in Zion and the kingdom (Obad. 1:10, 17–18, 21).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Obadiah', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — Edom Brought Down (Vv. 1–9)'), ('p', 'Edom, proud in the clefts of the rock, is brought down by the LORD (Obad. 1:1, 3–4).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — Edom’s Violence Against Jacob (Vv. 10–14)'), ('p', 'Edom is shamed for violence against his brother Jacob and for rejoicing over Judah (Obad. 1:10, 12).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — The Day of the LORD (Vv. 15–16)'), ('p', 'The day of the LORD is near upon all the heathen, and Edom’s reward returns upon his own head (Obad. 1:15).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — Zion’s Deliverance and the Kingdom (Vv. 17–21)'), ('p', 'The house of Jacob shall possess their possessions, and saviours go up on mount Zion (Obad. 1:17–18, 21).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Obadiah', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>A Vision Concerning Edom</b> — “Thus saith the Lord GOD concerning Edom” (Obad. 1:1).', '<b>Pride Brought Down</b> — “The pride of thine heart hath deceived thee” (Obad. 1:3).', '<b>Violence Against a Brother</b> — “For thy violence against thy brother Jacob shame shall cover thee” (Obad. 1:10).', '<b>The Day Near</b> — “The day of the LORD is near upon all the heathen” (Obad. 1:15).', '<b>Deliverance in Zion</b> — “Upon mount Zion shall be deliverance, and there shall be holiness” (Obad. 1:17).', '<b>The Kingdom the LORD’s</b> — “The kingdom shall be the LORD’s” (Obad. 1:21).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Obadiah', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how Obadiah points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Saviours on Zion</b> — “Saviours shall come up on mount Zion to judge the mount of Esau” is reported as written (Obad. 1:21).', '<b>The Kingdom the LORD’s</b> — The closing line stands within Israel’s kingdom hope (Obad. 1:21).', '<b>The Day of the LORD</b> — “The day of the LORD is near upon all the heathen” (Obad. 1:15) is reported beside Joel 1:15 and 3:14.'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Obadiah', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Obadiah speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Obadiah operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Obadiah in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Obadiah reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['Jacob as the brother wronged by Edom (Obad. 1:10)', 'the children of Judah in the day of their destruction (Obad. 1:12)', 'the house of Jacob and the house of Joseph (Obad. 1:18)', 'mount Zion and the kingdom the LORD’s (Obad. 1:17, 21)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Obadiah that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Obadiah contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['A Vision Concerning Edom — “Thus saith the Lord GOD concerning Edom.” (Obad. 1:1).', 'Violence Against Jacob — “thy violence against thy brother Jacob shame shall cover thee.” (Obad. 1:10).', 'Rejoicing Over Judah — “rejoiced over the children of Judah in the day of their destruction.” (Obad. 1:12).', 'Deliverance on Zion — “upon mount Zion shall be deliverance, and there shall be holiness.” (Obad. 1:17).', 'The House of Jacob — “the house of Jacob shall be a fire, and the house of Joseph a flame, and the house of Esau for stubble.” (Obad. 1:18).', 'The Kingdom the LORD’s — “saviours shall come up on mount Zion to judge the mount of Esau; and the kingdom shall be the LORD’s.” (Obad. 1:21).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Obadiah Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Obadiah and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Obadiah teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Obadiah', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['A Vision Concerning Edom', '“Thus saith the Lord GOD concerning Edom.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Obad. 1:1'], ['Violence Against Jacob', '“thy violence against thy brother Jacob shame shall cover thee.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Obad. 1:10'], ['Rejoicing Over Judah', '“rejoiced over the children of Judah in the day of their destruction.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Obad. 1:12'], ['Deliverance on Zion', '“upon mount Zion shall be deliverance, and there shall be holiness.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Obad. 1:17'], ['The House of Jacob', '“the house of Jacob shall be a fire, and the house of Joseph a flame, and the house of Esau for stubble.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Obad. 1:18'], ['The Kingdom the LORD’s', '“saviours shall come up on mount Zion to judge the mount of Esau; and the kingdom shall be the LORD’s.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Obad. 1:21']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Obadiah that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Obadiah teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['Pride Deceives', '“The pride of thine heart hath deceived thee.”', 'Obad. 1:3'], ['Brought Down', '“though thou exalt thyself as the eagle… thence will I bring thee down, saith the LORD.”', 'Obad. 1:4'], ['As Thou Hast Done', '“as thou hast done, it shall be done unto thee.”', 'Obad. 1:15']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Obadiah without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Obadiah in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Beware pride</b> — It deceives the heart (Obad. 1:3).', '<b>Remember the LORD brings down</b> — Whoever exalts himself (Obad. 1:4).', '<b>Treat others as you would be treated</b> — As thou hast done, it shall be done (Obad. 1:15).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Obadiah', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Obadiah in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['Edom Brought Down (Vv. 1–9) — Pride in the rock. <i>(Obad. 1:1, 3–4)</i>', 'Edom’s Violence (Vv. 10–14) — Against his brother Jacob. <i>(Obad. 1:10, 12)</i>', 'The Day of the LORD (Vv. 15–16) — Recompense. <i>(Obad. 1:15)</i>', 'Zion’s Deliverance (Vv. 17–21) — The kingdom the LORD’s. <i>(Obad. 1:17–18, 21)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Obadiah, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward humility, justice, and trust in the LORD’s reckoning without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Obadiah simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Obadiah stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]
